{"id": "sevt_01G3oDozvmErx5RUBtKE7rGS", "content": [{"text": "**Finding: checkout commit `584f83a` replaced a batch query with a per-row query, and checkout p99 rose from about 70 ms to 3.5\u20136 s starting at 14:13.**\n\n**Deploys (last 6h)**\n- `inventory` f696830 at 14:02:26. Checkout p99 was flat through 14:12, so this doesn't line up.\n- `checkout` 584f83a at 14:13:10, author grace, titled \"docs: clarify order summary comments\".\n- `checkout` dedba86 at 14:13:48, author erin, titled \"fix: batch order item lookups\".\n\n**Metrics**\n- `p99_latency_ms` (checkout): 63\u201380 ms through 14:12. It reads 3580 at 14:13 and stays between roughly 3.5k and 6.2k ms through 14:59. The gap from deploy to move is about 0\u20133 minutes.\n- `error_rate` (checkout): 0 until 14:20, then 0\u20131% from 14:21 on. Errors are small but started after the latency rise.\n\n**Diffs**\n- 584f83a is not a docs change. In `checkout/handlers.py`, `build_summary` used one `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` and grouped in memory. The commit replaces it with a loop that runs `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.order_id)` once per open order. This is the \"per-row query where there used to be a batch\" pattern from the runbook.\n- dedba86 adds migration `0042_lookup_index`, a concurrent index on `order_items(created_at)`. It doesn't match the `order_id` lookup path, and its message says \"batch order item lookups\", which the diff doesn't do. I don't think it caused the regression, but the title is misleading and it should be checked.\n\n**Log confirmation**\n- Batch queries (`order_id IN (?)`) appear at about 0\u201310 per minute before 14:13. The last batch query is at 14:13:07.\n- The per-row query (`order_id = ?`) first appears at 14:13:15, five seconds after 584f83a deployed. It runs 55\u2013177 times per minute from 14:13 through 14:52, one request producing a series of them.\n\n**Root cause:** `584f83a` \u2014 it replaced the batched `order_id__in` item fetch in `build_summary` with one `OrderItem` query per open order, so each checkout summary runs N extra DB round trips and p99 climbs into the seconds.\n\nConfidence is high on the mechanism because the diff and the log line up within seconds. Mitigation: revert 584f83a, or restore the batched query. Revert is the fastest path. Also, the commit message should be corrected, and the `0042` index should be reviewed separately.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:47:54.866063Z", "type": "agent.message"}
